    <md>
# Documentation

Stack turns a `.stk` file — HTML, CSS, JavaScript and Markdown together — into a plain static web page.

## Install

You need Node 18 or newer. From the Stack folder run:

```bash
node setup.js
```

The setup script checks Node, installs dependencies, registers the global `stk` command and installs the VS Code extension when `code` is available. Run `stk doctor` any time to verify your setup.

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `stk new [name]` | Creates a project. Options: `--theme <name>`, `--dep <spec>`, `--template <name>` |
| `stk dev [input]` | Dev server with live reload. Options: `-p, --port`, `-o, --open` |
| `stk build [input]` | Writes static files to `dist/`. Option: `-o, --out <dir>` |
| `stk themes` | Lists built-in themes with color swatches |
| `stk doctor` | Checks Node, the `stk` command and your project |

`input` is a `.stk` file or a folder of them. When omitted, Stack looks for `pages`, then `site`, then `index.stk`.

`stack` is an alias for `stk`.

## Pages and the page tag

Wrap each file in one `page` tag. Its attributes set the page title and look.

| Attribute | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `title` | Browser tab title |
| `description` | Search and social description |
| `theme` | Built-in or custom theme. Default `midnight` |
| `font` | Google Fonts family for body text |
| `heading` | Google Fonts family for headings |
| `favicon` | Path to the tab icon, e.g. `/logo.svg` |
| `og-image` | Image used for social previews |
| `lang` | Page language. Default `en` |

## Components

Each tag becomes themed, responsive HTML. Regular HTML works everywhere too.

| Tag | Attributes | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `navbar` | `brand`, `icon`, `links`, `cta`, `brand-href` | Sticky top bar. `links="Home:/, Docs:/docs"`. Children appear on the right |
| `hero` | none | Spacious intro section |
| `card` | `icon`, `title`, `href` | Bordered box. With `href` the whole card is a link |
| `alert` | `type`, `icon` | Callout. Types: `info`, `success`, `warning`, `danger` |
| `button` | `href`, `variant`, `icon`, `size` | Variants: `primary`, `secondary`, `ghost`, `none`. `size="sm"` is smaller |
| `badge` | `icon` | Small pill label |
| `icon` | `name`, `size` | A Lucide icon |
| `grid` | `cols` (1–6) | Equal columns that collapse on small screens |
| `row`, `stack` | none | Flex row, flex column |
| `themeswitch` | none | Theme picker, remembered between visits |
| `state` | any name | Declares reactive values |
| `footer` | social keys (`github`, `x`, `youtube`, …) | Site footer with optional social links |
| `md` | none | Markdown block (see below) |

```stk
<grid cols="3">
  <card icon="zap" title="Fast">Static output, no framework.</card>
  <card icon="palette" title="Themed">Ten themes included.</card>
  <alert type="success">Compiled without errors.</alert>
</grid>
```

## Icons

Stack uses [Lucide](https://lucide.dev/icons) via a CDN script added only to pages that use an icon. Pass a Lucide name to `icon` on cards, buttons, badges, alerts and the navbar, or use the `icon` tag:

```stk
<button icon="rocket">Launch</button>
<icon name="heart" size="20" />
```

Brand logos are not in Lucide; use the `footer` social attributes or plain text.

## State and events

Declare values with `state`, show them with curly braces, and change them from `on:` attributes.

```stk
<state count="0" name="friend" />

<p>Hello {name}</p>
<input on:input="name=e.target.value" />
<button on:click="count++">Clicked {count} times</button>
```

- Values are parsed as JSON when possible (`0`, `true`, `[1,2]`). Everything else is text.
- Any event works: `on:click`, `on:input`, `on:mouseover`.
- Handler code is plain JavaScript. State names act as variables; `e` is the event.
- Declare a name in `state` before using it.

## Themes

Ten themes ship with Stack: `midnight`, `daylight`, `paper`, `neon`, `ocean`, `forest`, `sunset`, `rose`, `dracula`, `nord`.

Set one with `theme="ocean"` on the page tag, or add a `themeswitch` so visitors can pick. See them all live on the [Themes page](/themes).

Every theme is a set of CSS variables: `bg`, `surface`, `surface-2`, `border`, `text`, `muted`, `accent`, `accent-2`, `accent-text`, `radius`, `shadow`, `font`, `heading-font`, `mono-font`.

Override any variable project-wide in `stack.config.json`:

```json
{ "vars": { "accent": "#ff5500", "radius": "8px" } }
```

Add a custom theme by creating `themes/mytheme.json` with the keys you want to change, then use `theme="mytheme"`. Put extra CSS in `stack.css` (loads after the theme).

## Markdown

Put Markdown between `<md>` and `</md>`. It becomes HTML and can sit inside any component.

- Headings, lists, tables and links work. Raw HTML is allowed.
- Fenced code is highlighted for `html`, `stk`, `js`, `css`, `json`, `bash` and `markdown`, with a copy button.
- Common leading spaces are stripped so you can indent the block.
- Curly-brace state is **not** applied inside Markdown.

## Multiple pages

Point Stack at a folder. Every `.stk` file becomes a page:

- `index.stk` → `/`
- `about.stk` → `/about`
- `guide/index.stk` → `/guide`

Files in a `public` folder are copied as-is (logos, images, fonts).

## Templates

Scaffold a ready-made site:

```bash
stk new my-site --template portfolio
stk new my-site --template saas
stk new my-site --template link-in-bio
```

Available names: `portfolio`, `blog`, `saas`, `link-in-bio`, `restaurant`, `resume`, `changelog`, `event`, `docs-hub`, `agency`, `newsletter`, `personal`.

Browse live previews and copyable source on the [Templates page](/templates).

## VS Code

`node setup.js` installs the extension when the `code` command exists. It adds highlighting for Markdown, CSS and JavaScript blocks plus snippets: `page`, `navbar`, `hero`, `card`, `button`, `alert`, `grid`, `state`, `icon`, `badge`, `md`.

Without the extension, add this to your settings:

```json
{ "files.associations": { "*.stk": "html" } }
```

## Deploy to Vercel

Vercel builds on every push and hosts the result. You need a free Vercel account and the project in a Git repository.

### Option 1 — this repository

`vercel.json` and the build script are already present.

1. Push the folder to a new GitHub repository.
2. In Vercel choose **Add New → Project** and import it.
3. Set **Framework Preset** to **Other**. Settings come from `vercel.json`:

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Build Command | `npm run build` |
| Output Directory | `dist` |
| Install Command | `npm install` |

4. Click **Deploy**. Later pushes redeploy; PRs get preview URLs.

### Option 2 — your own site

```bash
stk new my-site --dep github:YOURNAME/stack
cd my-site
git init && git add . && git commit -m "first commit"
```

Push to GitHub and import in Vercel as above. The `--dep` flag adds Stack to `package.json` so the build machine can install it and run `stk build`.

### Option 3 — Vercel CLI

```bash
npm install -g vercel
vercel login
vercel          # preview
vercel --prod   # production
```

### Custom domain

In the Vercel project go to **Settings → Domains** and add your domain. Vercel shows the DNS records; certificates are issued automatically.

### Clean URLs

`vercel.json` already enables clean URLs (`/docs` instead of `/docs.html`).

## Run it like a pro

```bash
stk doctor          # health check
stk themes          # list themes
stk build -o out    # custom output dir
stk dev -p 4000 -o  # custom port + open browser
```

Keep a `public/` folder for static assets. Use `stack.config.json` for project-wide variable overrides. Prefer one `.stk` file per route.

## Limits and roadmap

- No server-side rendering or API routes — Stack produces static HTML only.
- State is client-side and per-page; there is no shared store across pages.
- Custom components are plain HTML/CSS inside your `.stk` files (no plugin system yet).
- Planned: more templates, theme editor UI, and optional npm package publication.
